00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.589 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  the tokyo olympics made history even  before they began  they're the first games to ever be  postponed  they're also on track to be the most  expensive summer olympic games  ever so when japan won its bid to host  the olympics in 2013  it said that it would cost around 7.4  billion dollars but before the pandemic  the budget had risen by 70  to 12.6 billion dollars and that's not  really surprising  later japan's board of audit said the  cost would exceed 20 billion dollars  that was before pandemic related costs  set in  so the biggest costs for the olympics  are the facilities  the new national stadium here in tokyo  as well as  several other large facilities aquatic  center  and others that have been built for the  games and those cost over three billion  dollars  and there was about the same amount that  was spent on renovating  other facilities to bring them up to  standard for the games  so the whole thing in terms of venues  was around 7.5 billion  and the other big cost is the  operational budget for the olympics  it's around two billion dollars almost  every olympics ends up going over budget  bids are usually an optimistic estimate  what's different  in tokyo of course is that we've had  this one year delay  and we've also had a pandemic so the one  year  postponement meant that there are  additional costs incurred  for having to renegotiate contracts for  venues  for having to keep staff on for an  additional period  and various other things which added to  the overall budget  and the public risk from the pandemic  hasn't gone away so the tokyo olympics  are now proceeding with covet  countermeasures that require new costs  manpower and infrastructure together the  postponement and covid measures will add  an estimated 2.5 billion dollars to the  game's budget  according to event organizers on top of  those direct costs kovit is also taking  away much of japan's ability to recoup  what they've spent  ticket sales would be around 800 million  dollars  and now they'll be close to zero because  there'll be almost no spectators  and there'll be almost no income from  visitors of course  athletes and officials who have come  here from overseas might spend some  money but nothing like  the amount that the organizers were  hoping for from  spectators who would fill the stadiums  buy merchandise you know spend money in  hotels and restaurants and that could be  some of the economists say you know as  much as two billion dollars and now  that's lost  corporate sponsors have contributed  around 3 billion to the games  and the ioc has contributed 650 million  dollars to help cover the extra costs  resulting from the pandemic  still that leaves the bulk of the cost  of japan with little ability to recoup  in the short term but even the most  expensive summer olympics in history  pales in comparison to the overall cost  of the pandemic  japan has spent around 800 billion  dollars  on stimulus packages three of them  because of covid the pandemic is  obviously the  the most expensive thing to deal with  here it's not the olympics  but if the olympics makes the pandemic  worse  and there has to be you know new  stimulus measures  those could be far larger than the  actual budget of the olympics

